WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.471
This is CABINET,

00:00:04.551 --> 00:00:08.522
a framework for table question answering published
at ICLR 2024 by researchers at Adobe's

00:00:08.602 --> 00:00:12.573
MDSR Lab together with IIT Kharagpur and IIT Roorkee.

00:00:12.653 --> 00:00:16.624
Large language models reason over tables,

00:00:16.704 --> 00:00:20.676
but only a few cells usually matter for any given
question,

00:00:20.756 --> 00:00:24.727
and the irrelevant cells act as noise that hurts accuracy.

00:00:24.807 --> 00:00:28.778
CABINET tackles this by teaching the model to focus
on relevant table content and suppress the rest,

00:00:28.858 --> 00:00:32.829
rather than deleting parts of the table outright.

00:00:32.909 --> 00:00:36.880
It sets new state of the art on three challenging
table QA benchmarks.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.383
Tables organize information across rows and columns,

00:00:40.463 --> 00:00:45.618
but for any single question only a small number of
cells actually contain the answer.

00:00:45.698 --> 00:00:49.929
Everything else is irrelevant to that question and
behaves like noise.

00:00:50.009 --> 00:00:54.979
Large language models are known to be susceptible
to such distracting information,

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:57.073
so their table reasoning degrades,

00:00:57.153 --> 00:01:02.493
and the problem gets worse as tables grow larger
and carry even more irrelevant content.

00:01:02.573 --> 00:01:05.880
CABINET is built to address exactly this vulnerability.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.484
A natural way to reduce noise is to shrink the
table before answering,

00:01:10.564 --> 00:01:15.465
and methods like DATER do this by decomposing the
table into a smaller sub-table.

00:01:15.545 --> 00:01:21.061
The trouble is that hard decomposition is
unforgiving: if the wrong sub-table is extracted,

00:01:21.141 --> 00:01:27.148
useful information is permanently lost and the
reasoner answers incorrectly with no way to recover.

00:01:27.228 --> 00:01:34.035
CABINET argues for a softer approach that weighs
relevant parts higher without ever explicitly removing content,

00:01:34.115 --> 00:01:40.000
so the answering model retains access to the whole
table while being steered toward what matters.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.255
The paper contributes CABINET, short for Content
Relevance-Based Noise Reduction.

00:01:44.335 --> 00:01:48.210
It has two cooperating parts.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:52.165
First,

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:56.120
an Unsupervised Relevance Scorer assigns a soft
relevance weight to every table token

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.075
and is trained differentiably alongside the
question-answering model.

00:02:00.155 --> 00:02:04.030
Second,

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.985
a weakly-supervised module generates a parsing
statement describing which rows and columns matter,

00:02:08.065 --> 00:02:11.940
then highlights the corresponding cells to produce
a cell-based relevance signal.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.895
Together they let the model focus without
discarding content,

00:02:15.975 --> 00:02:19.850
and they deliver new state of the art on three
benchmarks along with stronger robustness to noise and to large tables.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:22.378
CABINET works in a sequence of steps.

00:02:22.458 --> 00:02:29.122
The table is flattened into a linear string with
header and row markers and embedded together with the question.

00:02:29.202 --> 00:02:32.494
An Unsupervised Relevance Scorer, a transformer encoder,

00:02:32.574 --> 00:02:36.287
reads this and predicts a relevance score for each
table token.

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:40.803
Because there are no ground-truth annotations for
which cells are relevant,

00:02:40.883 --> 00:02:45.741
relevance is treated as a latent variable estimated
through variational inference,

00:02:45.821 --> 00:02:51.040
and the encoder's representation space is shaped by
a clustering loss that groups tokens

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.906
into relevant and non-relevant,

00:02:52.986 --> 00:02:56.639
a separation loss that pushes the two cluster
centroids apart,

00:02:56.719 --> 00:03:02.601
and a sparsification loss that drives irrelevant
scores toward zero and relevant scores toward one.

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:06.575
Each table token's embedding is multiplied by its
relevance score,

00:03:06.655 --> 00:03:10.007
so noisy cells are softly suppressed rather than deleted,

00:03:10.087 --> 00:03:15.306
and the whole system trains end-to-end through the
answer-generation cross-entropy loss.

00:03:15.386 --> 00:03:18.918
In parallel a weakly-supervised Parsing Statement
Generator,

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:24.097
a fine-tuned Flan T5-xl bootstrapped from only
about three hundred manual annotations,

00:03:24.177 --> 00:03:28.733
writes a natural-language description of which rows
and columns are relevant,

00:03:28.813 --> 00:03:32.828
and a cell highlighter turns that into a cell-based
relevance score.

00:03:32.908 --> 00:03:39.090
The unsupervised and cell-based scores are linearly
combined into the final weight applied to the table.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:44.499
CABINET is evaluated on three challenging table
question-answering benchmarks.

00:03:44.579 --> 00:03:46.464
WikiTableQuestions, or WikiTQ,

00:03:46.544 --> 00:03:53.996
requires compositional reasoning over tables and
uses short one-to-two-word answers scored by exact-match accuracy.

00:03:54.076 --> 00:03:56.877
WikiSQL similarly uses exact-match accuracy.

00:03:56.957 --> 00:04:00.283
FeTaQA asks for long, free-form descriptive answers,

00:04:00.363 --> 00:04:02.445
which are scored with Sacre-BLEU.

00:04:02.525 --> 00:04:08.208
The authors additionally release a small dataset of
about three hundred manually written

00:04:08.288 --> 00:04:13.710
parsing statements used to bootstrap the
weakly-supervised cell-highlighting module.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:18.547
CABINET establishes new state of the art on all
three benchmarks.

00:04:18.627 --> 00:04:23.502
On WikiTQ it reaches sixty-nine point one percent
exact-match accuracy,

00:04:23.582 --> 00:04:29.086
outperforming the strongest baseline in each
category, including OmniTab, DATER,

00:04:29.166 --> 00:04:33.344
and fine-tuned Flan T5-xl by six point four, three
point two,

00:04:33.424 --> 00:04:36.694
and four point seven absolute percentage points,

00:04:36.774 --> 00:04:41.929
and it beats much larger GPT-3 and Codex based
in-context learning methods.

00:04:42.009 --> 00:04:45.768
On FeTaQA it achieves a Sacre-BLEU of forty point five,

00:04:45.848 --> 00:04:50.375
and on WikiSQL it reaches eighty-nine point five
percent accuracy,

00:04:50.455 --> 00:04:52.538
pushing past the previous best.

00:04:52.618 --> 00:04:58.820
Notably all of this is achieved with a compact five
hundred sixty million parameter model.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.936
Ablations confirm both components are needed.

00:05:02.016 --> 00:05:06.941
For the Unsupervised Relevance Scorer, applying
clustering, centroid-separation,

00:05:07.021 --> 00:05:13.949
and sparsification losses together lifts WikiTQ
accuracy from sixty point eight to sixty-five point six percent,

00:05:14.029 --> 00:05:16.452
whereas any subset gives little benefit,

00:05:16.532 --> 00:05:19.580
showing the three losses only help in combination.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.457
For the two relevance signals,

00:05:21.537 --> 00:05:26.775
fusing the unsupervised score at weight zero point
seven with the cell-based score at

00:05:26.855 --> 00:05:28.965
weight zero point three is optimal,

00:05:29.045 --> 00:05:31.843
giving sixty-nine point one percent on WikiTQ.

00:05:31.923 --> 00:05:37.600
Relying on the cell-based signal alone collapses
accuracy to thirty-seven point six percent,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:44.670
confirming the unsupervised scorer is the primary
driver and the parsing-statement module is a complementary aid.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.714
The headline numbers are sixty-nine point one
percent accuracy on WikiTQ,

00:05:49.794 --> 00:05:52.509
a Sacre-BLEU of forty point five on FeTaQA,

00:05:52.589 --> 00:05:56.083
and eighty-nine point five percent accuracy on WikiSQL,

00:05:56.163 --> 00:06:01.608
each a new state of the art achieved with just five
hundred sixty million parameters.

00:06:01.688 --> 00:06:02.908
Beyond raw accuracy,

00:06:02.988 --> 00:06:08.952
CABINET is markedly more robust: under table
perturbations such as row and column permutation

00:06:09.032 --> 00:06:12.656
and cell replacement it degrades far less than baselines,

00:06:12.736 --> 00:06:18.505
with roughly an eleven and a half percent smaller
performance drop than OmniTab on WikiTQ,

00:06:18.585 --> 00:06:21.690
and it holds its advantage as tables grow larger.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:27.895
The core lesson of CABINET is that you do not need
to cut a table down to answer questions about it.

00:06:27.975 --> 00:06:32.028
By softly weighting every cell according to a
learned relevance score,

00:06:32.108 --> 00:06:36.812
instead of hard-decomposing the table and risking
the loss of useful information,

00:06:36.892 --> 00:06:41.536
the model keeps full access to the data while being
steered toward what matters.

00:06:41.616 --> 00:06:47.263
Pairing a differentiable unsupervised relevance
scorer with a weakly-supervised parsing-statement

00:06:47.343 --> 00:06:51.810
cell highlighter yields both higher accuracy and
greater robustness to noise,

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:58.660
setting new state of the art on three table QA
benchmarks with a compact five hundred sixty million parameter model.
